WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.140
Want to watch something suspenseful, but not
in the mood for straight-up horror?

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:05.339
Netflix has you covered.

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:09.110
The streaming service has quite a few thrillers
that'll get your adrenaline going or send

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:10.390
a shiver up your spine.

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:13.880
But instead of watching some of the more mainstream
movies online, why not check out something

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.700
a little more obscure?

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:17.080
How about ...

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.570
Backcountry

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:22.210
Like Deliverance or The Blair Witch Project
before it, Backcountry will make you think

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:24.189
twice before taking a walk in the woods.

00:00:24.189 --> 00:00:27.890
Like its forebearers, this 2015 flick features
folks who decide to spend a weekend in the

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:30.260
forest, even though they're certified city
slickers.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:31.600
"Where is it?"

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:33.800
"In the car."

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.210
"Why would you do that?"

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:39.760
"I didn't want you on that thing the whole
time we were here."

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.750
Soon, the two find themselves map-less, phone-less
and lost in the woods, and it doesn't help

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:48.090
matters knowing there's another hiker nearby
who seems kind of dangerous.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:51.269
But really, he's the least of their problems,
considering they're also being stalked by

00:00:51.269 --> 00:00:52.860
a man-eating black bear …

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.420
Blue Caprice

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:02.440
On October 2, 2002, a man was shot to death
in the parking lot of a Maryland grocery store.

00:01:02.449 --> 00:01:06.200
It was the first killing in a three-week long
murder spree that would claim ten lives in

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:07.740
the Washington D.C. area.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:10.870
The terror came to an end when police arrested
two snipers who'd been shooting their way

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:15.479
across the U.S. for months, leaving a total
of 17 bodies in their wake.

00:01:15.479 --> 00:01:19.110
2013's ripped-from-the-headlines Blue Caprice
takes place in the months leading up to the

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:23.439
shootings, focusing on the twisted relationship
between the snipers, John Allen Muhammad and

00:01:23.439 --> 00:01:24.909
Lee Boyd Malvo.

00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:29.130
Abandoned by his mom, the teenage Malvo gravitates
toward the manipulative Muhammad, who takes

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:30.409
the boy under his wing.

00:01:30.409 --> 00:01:34.380
But in exchange for a father figure, Malvo
agrees to help Muhammad enact his homicidal

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:34.920
plan.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:42.020
"I did it"

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:43.500
Cold in July

00:01:43.509 --> 00:01:46.909
It starts off like a typical revenge flick
about a man caught in the crosshairs of an

00:01:46.909 --> 00:01:47.909
angry ex-con.

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:52.189
But about midway through, 2014's Cold in July
takes such a hard left turn that it becomes

00:01:52.189 --> 00:01:53.700
a completely different beast.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.070
"Don't you have any mirrors on this damn Pinto?

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:58.280
Do you know how to use it?

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:00.300
Who's gonna pay for it?

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:01.960
We could split it ..."

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.170
Richard Dane, played by Dexter's Michael C.
Hall, is in a tricky situation after accidentally

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:09.289
killing a burglar who's broken into his home.

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:12.510
Things get worse when he discovers that the
crook's dad is one bad dude.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:16.790
At first, Cold in July plays like a cat-and-mouse
game between the two, but our hero soon realizes

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:20.760
there's a conspiracy afoot, and he's a pawn
in a much larger scheme.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:21.760
The Double

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:25.510
If you're a fan of Jesse Eisenberg, well,
2013's The Double is definitely the movie

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:28.030
for you: it's got two Eisenbergs for the price
of one.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:32.319
Based on the novella by Dostoyevsky, The Double
follows a young office worker named Simon

00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:34.470
James, a guy beaten down by the system.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:39.030
He's nervous, shy, and ignored by everyone,
including Hannah, the girl of his dreams.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.140
"Hannah, the creepy guy's here again."

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:44.620
"Hi, Creepy Guy."

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:48.560
His life gets turned upside down when a man
named James Simon becomes his co-worker.

00:02:48.569 --> 00:02:51.870
James is Simon's exact opposite in almost
every way.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:54.209
Simon is introverted, while James is outgoing.

00:02:54.209 --> 00:02:56.900
Simon is full of self-doubt, whereas James
is confidence personified.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:02.100
Of course, the two men do share one eerie
similarity: they look exactly alike, a fact

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:03.990
that no one else seems to notice.

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:08.100
Unfortunately, the creepy resemblance allows
James to completely invade Simon's life, but

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:11.409
everybody's got a breaking point, and Simon
eventually decides it's time to stand his

00:03:11.409 --> 00:03:12.600
ground, whatever the consequences.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:14.860
"He stole my face!"

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:16.360
"You need to leave."

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:18.000
"No, you do.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:18.840
Get off of me!

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:19.920
"Arrgh!"

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:22.280
Frailty

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:27.580
The late great actor Bill Paxton also dabbled
in directing, with a 2005 golfing flick starring

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:32.590
Shia LaBeouf called The Greatest Game Ever
Played, and the 2001 thriller Frailty, a terrifying

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:36.569
portrait of religion gone wrong that dances
on the fine line between "thriller" and "horror."

00:03:36.569 --> 00:03:40.310
In addition to calling the shots behind the
camera, Paxton also played a loving father

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:44.610
who becomes an axe murderer after God supposedly
orders him to rid the world of demons…demons

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:45.799
that look like humans.

00:03:45.799 --> 00:03:48.209
But he isn't going to fight the forces of
evil by himself.

00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:51.810
Believing he's truly a divine warrior, he
recruits his two sons—one who's totally

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.510
on board, and another who think his dad has
gone mad.

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:57.069
"We don't fear these demons.

00:03:57.069 --> 00:03:58.709
We destroy them.

00:03:58.709 --> 00:04:03.170
We pick them up, one by one, and we pitch
them out of this world."

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:07.150
While ultimately more thriller than horror,
be warned: the film does involve a whole lot

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:10.790
of head-chopping, body-burying, and disturbing
scenes of child abuse.

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:12.420
"God saw you.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:13.180
"Dad no!

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:14.180
Please!"

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:18.180
"And you can't escape God's wrath!"

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:21.270
The Imposter

00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:24.640
A true story of a creepy con man that will
leave your mind reeling when the credits start

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:29.860
to role, 2012's The Imposter is the rare documentary
that also works as a pulse-pounding thriller.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:34.320
"I didn't give a damn what other people were
thinking and what they were feeling.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.120
I care about myself."

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:40.290
The less you know about the part documentary,
part staged thriller the better—but here's

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:44.590
a taste: The Imposter tells the story of a
French criminal who posed as a missing Texas

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:48.430
teen and convinced the boy's family that he
was their long-lost relative—even though

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:51.940
he was too old, had a French accent, and the
wrong hair and eye color.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:52.940
How?

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:54.480
You'll have to watch and find out.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:58.540
"A story so bizarre it's hard to believe it's
true."

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:01.420
"I mean, seriously?"

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:02.580
The Invitation

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:05.790
We've all been invited to some pretty weird
parties, but none compare to the ultra-creepy

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:07.740
affair in 2016's The Invitation.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:12.390
It all starts when quiet, brooding Will shows
up for dinner at his ex-wife's house.

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:15.830
She's back in the States after getting remarried
in Mexico and wants to get together with the

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:19.490
old gang—including Will, who's still struggling
with their loss of their son.

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:23.120
So maybe that's why Will starts acting so
strangely once he arrives at the party.

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:25.830
Or maybe there's something rotten going on
in the Hollywood Hills.

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:28.710
"We chose you, because you matter to us."

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:29.710
"You chose me?"

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:31.750
"To be here, tonight."

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:33.800
After all, why are there bars on all the windows?

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:35.090
Why is the front door locked?

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:38.550
As the evening unwinds, Will finds himself
battling a sneaking suspicion that something

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:42.650
terrible is about to happen, all while battling
his own emotional breakdown.

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:44.860
We Need to Talk About Kevin

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:50.300
The 2011 domestic thriller We Need to Talk
About Kevin focuses on Tilda Swinton's Eva

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:51.300
and her son Kevin.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:53.840
To say they don't get along would be an understatement.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:55.220
"I don't give a rat's ass."

00:05:57.910 --> 00:06:01.420
Kevin is an absolute monster — a budding
psychopath who charms the world into thinking

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:02.620
he's a normal kid.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:06.840
But when he's alone with his mom, that's when
we see the teen's true sadistic side, a darkness

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:08.990
that's been growing ever since Kevin was an
infant.

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:13.520
"Then finally once you've sucked up that entire
bottle of wine, you can go all gooey-eyed

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:15.920
and say how nice it is to spend quality time
together."

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:23.090
His downward spiral divides Eva and her husband
as it becomes increasingly clear what Kevin

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:25.070
is truly capable of.

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:28.480
"There is no point.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:29.400
That's the point."

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:32.180
Thanks for watching!

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:34.520
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:36.650
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

